WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.389
Hey this is Presh Talwalkar. I received this

00:00:03.389 --> 00:00:05.310
problem by email because it's going

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:07.919
viral on Facebook with thousands of

00:00:07.919 --> 00:00:10.410
people arguing about the correct answer.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:14.960
How smart are you? A guy walks into a

00:00:14.969 --> 00:00:16.560
store and steals a hundred-dollar bill

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:19.170
from the register without the owner's

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:22.590
knowledge. He then buys seventy dollars

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:24.330
worth of good using the hundred-dollar

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:27.119
bill and the owner gives thirty dollars

00:00:27.119 --> 00:00:31.119
in change. How much money did the owner lose?

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:39.060
Was it $30, $70, $100, $130, $170, or $200?

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:42.060
Can you figure it out?

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.340
Give this problem a try and when you're

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:47.500
ready keep watching the video for the correct answer.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:54.629
In some math problems,

00:00:54.629 --> 00:00:55.800
and in this story,

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.690
it helps to break the problem down into

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:01.500
different parts so at first we'll only

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.930
consider the first part of the story.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:06.780
A guy walks into a store and steals a

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:08.369
hundred-dollar bill from the register

00:01:08.369 --> 00:01:11.000
without the owner's knowledge.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:13.170
At this point how much does the owner lose?

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.620
Everyone agrees the owner loses a hundred dollars.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:22.590
The owner is at minus 100 from the initial

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:27.930
part of the story. So now let's

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:30.360
separately consider just  the second

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:31.530
part of the story.

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:34.950
Imagine someone buys seventy dollars worth

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:37.829
of goods using a hundred-dollar bill and

00:01:37.829 --> 00:01:40.220
the owner gives thirty dollars in change.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:42.600
How much would you say the owner has

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:46.080
lost in just this part of the story?

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:49.060
Well if someone buys seventy dollars worth

00:01:49.079 --> 00:01:51.750
of good using a hundred-dollar bill you

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:53.250
would expect the owner to give thirty

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:55.439
dollars in change. In fact the owner is

00:01:55.439 --> 00:02:00.210
overseeing this transaction. So the owner

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:02.820
here is not losing any money.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:08.660
The owner is you could say zero in this transaction.

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:12.000
So now we put these two

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.420
stories together

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:17.319
the total loss will be the sum of these

00:02:17.319 --> 00:02:20.260
two parts of the story so we can add

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:24.280
-100 and 0 to get the total loss is

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:27.340
$100 the owner is negative one hundred

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:29.440
dollars from before the story started

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:34.000
the correct answer is the owner lost $100.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.110
Now there's one technicality if you

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:40.090
want to really overthink this problem

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:42.730
and go into the accounting details.

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:45.099
Now when someone buys seventy dollars worth of

00:02:45.099 --> 00:02:48.040
goods in a store it's not because the

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:51.000
goods are worth seventy dollars to the owner.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.040
What happens here is the owner is

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:56.080
getting a hundred-dollar bill and gives

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:57.970
back thirty dollars in change but the

00:02:57.970 --> 00:03:00.549
goods have some value V. This is usually

00:03:00.549 --> 00:03:04.090
going to be less than seventy dollars so

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:06.459
you could say the owner's actually losing

00:03:06.459 --> 00:03:08.799
the value of the goods but then getting

00:03:08.799 --> 00:03:10.540
a net seventy dollars because it's a

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:12.489
hundred-dollar bill minus thirty dollars

00:03:12.489 --> 00:03:15.100
in change but here the owner loses V

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:18.370
minus 70 or you could say the owner is

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:21.519
70 minus V in terms of the cash position

00:03:21.519 --> 00:03:25.450
so if you were to take these two stories

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:28.630
now you add up the result the owner

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:31.660
ultimately loses thirty dollars plus the

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:34.420
value V of the goods

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:36.730
so if we assume the value V is seventy

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:38.769
dollars which you say seventy dollars

00:03:38.769 --> 00:03:40.390
worth of goods then you would say the

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:42.730
owner lost a hundred dollars. So you can

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:44.829
still arrive at the same answer of a hundred

00:03:44.829 --> 00:03:46.180
dollars but this is a little more

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:49.000
accurate in terms of pure accounting.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.970
So why are so many people getting this

00:03:51.970 --> 00:03:53.230
problem wrong?

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:55.329
Well one of the common answers is there

00:03:55.329 --> 00:03:56.890
is a two-hundred-dollar loss to the

00:03:56.890 --> 00:03:59.680
owner. Many people think this is because

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:02.859
in the first part of the story the owner

00:04:02.859 --> 00:04:05.170
loses a hundred dollars that's stolen and

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:07.000
then in the second part of the story the

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:09.100
owner loses another hundred dollars

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:10.750
because the owner is giving up seventy

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:12.819
dollars worth of goods and thirty dollars

00:04:12.819 --> 00:04:16.269
in change. So the mistake here is that it's

00:04:16.269 --> 00:04:17.799
not taking into account the owner is

00:04:17.799 --> 00:04:21.160
also getting a hundred-dollar bill so

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:23.080
when you take that other part of the

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:26.300
story where you get the hundred-dollar bill

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:29.620
that will adjust the total by +100 and when

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:31.090
you do that, that means the owner

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:34.250
ultimately lost a hundred dollars in total.

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:37.920
Another common answer is that the owner

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:40.330
had a $130 loss. This is

00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:41.949
from the stolen hundred-dollar bill

00:04:41.949 --> 00:04:44.050
and the thirty dollars in change.

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:46.540
Well the problem with this answer is

00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:48.220
it's not taking into account that the owner

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:50.229
also gave up seventy dollars in goods

00:04:50.229 --> 00:04:53.560
but also got a hundred-dollar bill so

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:55.180
when you take into account these

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:58.389
transactions the net result would be the

00:04:58.389 --> 00:05:00.460
owner losing $100 which is

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:04.300
negative 100. So to summarize most of the

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:06.850
incorrect responses either didn't add or

00:05:06.850 --> 00:05:08.830
subtract numbers correctly or they

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:10.120
didn't take into account every

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:13.030
transaction. It seems the fact that in

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:14.680
the second half of the story that person

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:16.630
used the stolen dollar bill somehow

00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:19.140
people don't treat that as a genuine hundred-dollar bill.

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:21.340
That is a hundred dollars that the owner's getting back.

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:23.580
So when you take into account every single

00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:26.080
transaction and you treated correctly as

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:29.320
either a loss or gain for the owner you

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:32.380
end up that the owner lost a hundred dollars.

